**Approximate Pareto front** (non-dominated, both objectives):
- **High modulus, lower pore fraction**: 936 bar / 0.27 (run1, single specimen, high CV, wrinkles — not validated)
- **High modulus, moderate pore fraction**: 658 bar / 0.31 (run6, clean membrane — single run)
- **Moderate modulus, high pore fraction**: 421–468 bar / 0.40–0.44 (18-15deg-600s-NoN2 series)
- **Balanced**: 592 bar / 0.31 (18-12deg-90s-N2-1800s, clean, single run)

### Validation Status

- **17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s**: 6 runs total. Run1 (936 bar, 0.27) — single specimen, SD=0, likely only 1 fit survived, very uncertain. Run2 (675, 0.30), Run6 (659, 0.31) are more reliable. Runs 4 & 5 had no modulus data (all fits failed). The reproducible mean across runs with data is roughly 650–700 bar with pore fraction ~0.28–0.31. This candidate has been run many times but the high-modulus run1 is not reproducible — the confirmed reproducible performance is ~650–700 bar / 0.29–0.31.

- **18-12deg-90s-N2-1800s**: Only 1 run with both objectives (run1: 592 bar, 0.31, clean). Run2 had only 1 fit survive (349 bar, 0.47 — very different, high thickness SD). This candidate is **not validated** — the two runs disagree substantially.

- **17p5-15deg-200s-NoN2-1800s**: 1 run, 367 bar / 0.38, clean membrane. Not validated.

- **18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s**: 5 runs. Runs 1 (476 bar, 0.39), 2 (339 bar, 0.32), 3 (421 bar, 0.44), 4 (430 bar, 0.36), 5 (468 bar, 0.40). Mean modulus ~427 bar, mean pore fraction ~0.38. This is reasonably reproducible but modulus is moderate.

### Key Observations

The most interesting unvalidated candidate for **both high modulus and high pore fraction** is **18-12deg-90s-N2-1800s** (run1: 592 bar, 0.31 — clean membrane). Run2 gave 349 bar / 0.47 with a wrinkled, incomplete-coverage membrane — the disagreement is likely due to membrane quality differences. A third run would clarify whether the run1 result is reproducible.

However, there's also an underexplored region: **polymer_wt ~17.5–18, bath_temp ~12–14°C, N2=true, moderate wait time** with pullcast_speed varied. The 18-12deg run used speed=10; the 18.5-12deg run used speed=8. The data suggest bath_temp ~12°C with 18 wt% polymer and N2 can give good modulus. But we haven't tried **lower pullcast speed (e.g., 6–8 mm/s) with 17.5 wt% polymer at bath_temp ~12°C** — this could push pore fraction higher while maintaining modulus, since lower polymer concentration tends to increase porosity and lower bath temp tends to increase modulus.

### Decision

The **18-12deg-90s-N2-1800s** candidate (run1: 592 bar / 0.31, clean) is a Pareto-relevant point that is **not independently validated** — run2 gave very different results, likely due to membrane quality. Per policy, I should validate this candidate. The parameters are within bounds and feasible.

**Validation repeat**: polymer_wt=18.0, bath_temp=12, mixing_temp=65, pullcast_speed=10, nitrogen=true, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=90, nips_bath_wait_time=1800.

**Expected benefit**: If reproducible, 592 bar / 0.31 would be a confirmed Pareto point — better modulus than the 18-15deg-600s-NoN2 series (which gives ~430 bar / 0.38) and better pore fraction than the 17p5-18deg series (~650 bar / 0.29–0.31). Confirming or refuting this point will clarify whether the 12°C bath with 18 wt% polymer is a reliable route to balanced performance.

